WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:07.720
In the deepest darkest depths of the frigid
North Atlantic Ocean lies a 1.2 square kilometre

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:14.070
island called Hans Island and Canada and Denmark
just can't help but put their sticky "Hans"

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.259
all over it.

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:19.970
Hans Island lies directly in the middle of
Nares Strait, which separates Canada from

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:25.890
Greenland, Greenland as you may know is an
overseas territory owned by Denmark.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:31.140
Since 1930 there has been an ongoing dispute
between the Canadian and Danish governments

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.600
over who owns this small, barren island in
the middle of absolutely nowhere.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.030
You see, under international law, which is
just fancy terminology for The Pirate's Code,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:47.339
all countries have the right to claim any
land within 12 miles of their coastline.

00:00:47.339 --> 00:00:52.780
Since the Nares Strait, which Hans Island
sits in, is only 22 miles wide, that puts

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:58.179
the island within 12 miles of both Greenland
and Canada, meaning they both have a claim

00:00:58.179 --> 00:00:59.179
to it.

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:01.530
So, who actually owns it?

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.939
Well, I'm sorry Canada, but the island was
officially ruled to be Danish territory by

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:12.009
the Permanent Court of International Justice
of the League of Nations in 1933.

00:01:12.009 --> 00:01:17.189
However, shortly afterwards the League of
Nations was abolished, which made them really

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:19.790
regret calling it the Permanent Court.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.970
It was replaced with the United Nations.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:28.429
After which Denmark's claim to Hans Island
was kind of forgotten by every other nation,

00:01:28.429 --> 00:01:30.869
since it no longer carried any weight.

00:01:30.869 --> 00:01:36.969
Canada subsequently told Denmark to keep their
"Hans" off Hans Island and the two have been

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:39.549
bickering about it ever since.

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:45.030
Luckily Canada and Denmark are fairly peaceful
nations and this long running dispute is often

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.939
settled in a humorous manner.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:53.899
In 1984 Denmark's minister of Greenland affairs
visited Hans Island and stuck a Danish flag

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:54.899
in it.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:59.999
At the base of the flag he left a note that
read "Welcome to the Danish Island", he also

00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:02.049
left a bottle of brandy.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:06.960
This marked the start of the so-called "whiskey
war" between the two nations.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.430
Ever since that day, the two nations' militaries
have been randomly sailing to Hans Island,

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:18.290
replacing the other nation's flag with their
own and leaving a bottle of whiskey and a

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.150
sign saying either "Welcome to Canada" or
"Welcome to Denmark".

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.680
Danish ambassador Peter Jensen said that whenever
the Danish visit the island they leave a bottle

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.160
of Schnapps and the Canadians apparently leave
a bottle of Canadian Club whiskey.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.260
It's like the coldest cold-war that ever happened.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.620
This may all seem a bit silly for two countries
arguing over land, but to me it seems a lot

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.680
more mature than nuclear warheads, and certainly
a lot funnier too.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.940
But even Canada and Denmark recognise that
it may not be all Schnapps and giggles forever.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:58.829
The two nations see it as a friendly but long-standing
dispute and they both admit it has potential

00:02:58.829 --> 00:03:04.470
to cause friction in the future, especially
since melting ice caps makes the arctic circle

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:09.360
an ever increasingly attractive place for
trade routes between certain countries.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.579
So, there are currently plans to turn Hans
island into a condominum, which would mean

00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:19.739
it is collectively owned by both countries,
so basically they have to share and no more

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:41.719
whiskey or sarcastic signs.

